<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minh Mang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1841">1841</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minh Mang was the second emperor of the Nguyen dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, ruling from 1820 to 1841. He centralised the administration on Chinese models, renamed the country Dai Nam and persecuted Catholic missionaries and converts. In 1832 he abolished the last autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> principality of Thuan Thanh, ending the political history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minh-mang.jpg" alt="Minh Mang" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, there is a line below the picture in the book that says: Drawn by a Chinese. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minh_Mang.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Minh Mạng was born Nguyễn Phúc Đảm on 25 May 1791 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-dinh">Gia Định</a>, during the Second Tây Sơn–Nguyễn War. He was the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyễn Phúc Ánh</a>, the future Emperor Gia Long, and his second wife Trần Thị Đang. At the age of three he was taken in and raised by Gia Long's first wife, the Empress Thừa Thiên, under a written agreement between her and Gia Long. After her death in 1814 Gia Long insisted that Đảm, as her son, perform the funeral duties instead of her grandson, despite opposition from mandarins. In 1816 he appointed Đảm his heir apparent, and the prince began assisting him with documents and affairs of state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He became emperor on 14 February 1820. A cholera epidemic reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> that summer and removed 206,835 taxpayers from the registers. He continued his father's reorganisation of the administration, with highways, a postal service, public food storehouses and monetary and agrarian reforms. He further centralised government, and in 1839 introduced salaries and pensions for princes and mandarins in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> estates. About 200 rebellions were recorded in his twenty-year reign. The largest, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-van-khoi">Lê Văn Khôi</a>, broke out in July 1833 after Minh Mạng had the late viceroy Lê Văn Duyệt posthumously indicted; Khôi gained Siamese support, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> fell in September 1835, when rebel commanders were put to death.</p><p>Minh Mạng was wary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and refused the trade and diplomatic offers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In 1825 he banned Christian missionaries, and between 1833 and 1838 seven were sentenced to death, among them Pierre Borie, Joseph Marchand and Jean-Charles Cornay. He annexed Muang Phuan as Tran Ninh prefecture in 1832, completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, and incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> as the province of Tây Thành in 1834, then tried to impose Vietnamese culture there. A war with Siam over Cambodia was fought in 1833 and 1834. In 1827 he barred recently arrived Chinese of the Qing period from the bureaucracy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> monopoly and foreign trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Minh Mạng died on 20 January 1841. He was known for his scholarship, his poetry and his close attention to detail, which astonished his contemporaries, and he took an interest in Western mechanics, weaponry and navigation. He is reported to have fathered 142 children by 43 wives, and he set a poem from which his descendants' generation names were drawn. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thieu-tri">Thiệu Trị</a> succeeded him. Later European demands on the pretext of the treatment of Catholic priests led, in the account given, to gunboat diplomacy and eventually to French colonisation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minh Mạng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Catholicism, spread by missionaries since the seventeenth century, had a few hundred thousand adherents; Gia Long tolerated it out of gratitude to Pigneau, but <b>Minh Mang</b> issued edicts against it from 1833, and missionaries and Vietnamese Catholics were executed under him and under Tu Duc.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Ho Chi Minh City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Died in Huế, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.78,106.7&amp;year=1823" aria-label="Ho Chi Minh City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7489 1762 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Minh Mang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7939 2032h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7939 2032h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.78,106.7&amp;year=1823">What was here in 1823? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
